== Baggy Pant Appearance (SAM)
=== Clinical features of SAM
- Severe wasting
- Oedema
- Irritability or apathy
- Hypothermia
- Hepatomegaly (kwashiorkor)
- Skin changes
- Hair changes
- Diarrhoea
- Dehydration
=== Skin changes
- Kwashiorkor: “Flaky paint” dermatitis with hyperpigmented patches that peel off, erosions, fissures, often over pressure areas (buttocks, groin, behind knees)
- Marasmus: Dry, loose, atrophic skin, no typical rash
=== Baggy pant appearance
- Loose, hanging skin folds over buttocks and upper thighs due to loss of gluteal fat and muscle
=== Management (10 points)
- Treat/prevent hypoglycaemia
- Treat/prevent hypothermia
- Treat/prevent dehydration
- Correct electrolyte imbalance
- Treat and prevent infection
- Correct micronutrient deficiency
- Start feeding cautiously (F-75)
- Achieve catch-up growth (F-100)
- Provide sensory stimulation and emotional support
- Prepare for discharge and follow-up after recovery
=== Deworming in SAM
- Albendazole: 400 mg single dose for >2 years; 200 mg for 1–2 years
- Mebendazole: 500 mg single dose or 100 mg BD × 3 days
- In SAM, deworming is given in phase 2 (rehabilitation), not during the acute phase
== Measles - Maculopapular Rash
=== Signs and symptoms
- Maculopapular rash (generalized)
- Conjunctivitis
- Coryza
=== Differential diagnosis
- Dengue
- Rubella
- EBV
- CMV
=== Hallmark
- Koplik's spot on the buccal mucosa
=== IMCI classification
- Measles: now or within last 3 months
- Measles + eye/mouth complications
- Severe complicated measles:
  - Any danger sign
  - Corneal clouding
  - Deep/extensive mouth ulcer
=== Treatment
- Vitamin A
- Eye infection: Tetracycline, eye ointment
- Mouth ulcer: gentian violet
- Appropriate antibiotics
=== Complications
- Diarrhoea
- Bacterial pneumonia
- Otitis media
- SSPE (Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis) — 7 years after infection
=== Vitamin A Deficiency
==== Clinical features: ocular and extraocular
===== Ocular
- XN: night blindness
- XIA: conjunctival xerosis
- XIB: Bitot’s spot
- X2: corneal xerosis
- X3A: corneal ulceration, < 1/3 of corneal surface
- X3B: corneal ulceration, > 1/3 of corneal surface
- XS: corneal scar
- XF: retinal lesion (white, xerophthalmic fundi)
===== Extraocular
- Growth retardation
- Increased susceptibility to infections
- Dry, scaly skin
- Alopecia or hair changes
- Delayed wound healing
- Squamous metaplasia of respiratory epithelium
===== Associated features in Kwashiorkor
- Age: 1–3 years
- Edema: Present
- Wasting: Less obvious
- Skin changes: More frequent
- Hair changes: Present
- Appetite: Poor
- Hepatomegaly: Present
- Serum albumin: Low
- Anemia: Common
- Liver biopsy: Fatty change
==== Treatment
- Therapeutic dosing for clinical vitamin A deficiency (WHO guidance):
  - < 6 months: 50,000 IU PO once daily for 2 days
  - 6–11 months: 100,000 IU PO once daily for 2 days
  - ≥ 12 months: 200,000 IU PO once daily for 2 days
  - Repeat dose after 4 weeks in severe cases per local protocol

==== Prevention
- Routine prophylactic supplementation follows national schedules; encourage breastfeeding and vitamin A–rich foods (dark green leafy vegetables, orange fruits, liver)
- In measles, give therapeutic vitamin A to all children with measles as above
== Girl er Face - Measles
=== Key points
=== Causative organism
Measles virus (paramyxovirus family)
=== Presentation
High fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and Koplik spots.
Rash is maculopapular, starts on face and behind ears, then spreads downwards.
=== Questions
- Causative organism of measles?
- Transmission mode?
- Definition of a measles case?
- Clinical features?

=== Clinical features
Fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, Koplik spots, rash.
Rash distribution begins at hairline/face and spreads cephalocaudally and centrifugally.

=== Treatment
Supportive care, hydration, antipyretics, isolate the patient.
Vitamin A supplementation for all children with measles.
Treat bacterial superinfection if present.

=== Prevention
Measles vaccine as part of MMR schedule.
Routine immunization at 9-12 months and 15-18 months, plus catch-up doses.
Isolation of cases and post-exposure prophylaxis for susceptible contacts.

=== Vitamin A dosing in measles
< 6 months: 50,000 IU PO once daily for 2 days.
6–12 months: 100,000 IU PO once daily for 2 days.
> 12 months: 200,000 IU PO once daily for 2 days.

=== IMCI classification with treatment
Measles without complications: supportive home care and vitamin A.
Measles with eye/mouth complications: outpatient treatment with eye care, mouth care, antibiotics if needed.
Severe complicated measles: inpatient care, IV fluids, antibiotics, vitamin A.

=== Mumps
=== Mumps organism
Mumps virus (paramyxovirus)
=== Mumps clinical features
Parotitis, fever, orchitis, meningitis, pancreatitis.
=== Mumps treatment
Supportive care, analgesics, hydration, warm/cold packs.
=== Mumps prevention
MMR vaccine.

== Chicken Pox - Papular Rash / Vesicular Rash
=== Key points
=== Organism
Varicella zoster virus.
=== Transmission
Respiratory droplets and direct contact with vesicle fluid.
=== Rash appearance
Crops of pruritic vesicles on erythematous base in different stages.
=== Complications
Bacterial skin infection, pneumonia, cerebellitis, encephalitis, thrombocytopenia.
=== Treatment
Supportive care with hydration, antipyretics and antihistamines.
Acyclovir for high-risk children or severe disease.
Keep skin clean and nails short to prevent secondary infection.
=== Prevention
Varicella vaccine in routine schedule.
Post-exposure prophylaxis for vulnerable contacts when indicated.
  - Varicella vaccine in routine schedule
  - Post-exposure prophylaxis for vulnerable contacts when indicated

== Purpuric Rash - ITP, Dengue
=== Dengue Fever
- Clinical features
  - High fever, severe headache, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, arthralgia
  - Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, rash, bleeding manifestations
- Rash type and distribution
  - Initial flushing rash with islands of white
  - Maculopapular or petechial rash on face, trunk, limbs
  - Palms and soles may be involved
- Warning signs
  - Severe abdominal pain / tenderness
  - Persistent vomiting
  - Mucosal bleeding
  - Lethargy, restlessness
  - Hepatomegaly
  - Fluid accumulation (ascites, pleural effusion)
  - Rapid drop in platelet count, rising hematocrit
- Investigation
  - CBC with hematocrit and platelet count
  - LFTs, electrolytes, coagulation profile
  - Dengue NS1 antigen, IgM/IgG serology
  - Tourniquet test if available
- Day 3 follow-up investigation
  - Repeat CBC with hematocrit and platelets
  - Monitor liver enzymes and electrolytes
  - Evaluate fluid status and urine output
- Most important investigation
  - CBC with hematocrit and platelet count for early dengue monitoring
- Management of DF, DHF, DSS
  - DF: outpatient care, paracetamol, hydration, avoid NSAIDs/aspirin
  - DHF: careful fluid management, monitor for plasma leakage, supportive care
  - DSS: urgent resuscitation with isotonic crystalloids or colloids, ICU care, blood products if needed
- How to recognise deterioration
  - Narrowing pulse pressure, hypotension, tachycardia
  - Rising hematocrit with falling platelet count
  - New pleural effusion or ascites
  - Persistent vomiting or severe abdominal pain
- Bedside examination: temperature, pulse, BP, capillary refill
  - Check for signs of shock and dehydration
- Tests required in DSS
  - Serial hematocrit, platelets, electrolytes, LFTs, coagulation studies
  - Chest X-ray or ultrasound for fluid leak if indicated
- ITP
=== Overview
- Acute immune thrombocytopenic purpura is common after viral infection in children
- Presents with petechiae, purpura, mucosal bleeding, and isolated thrombocytopenia
=== Diagnosis
- Platelet count typically < 100,000/mm3
- Normal hemoglobin and white count
- Peripheral smear shows few platelets, otherwise normal cells
=== Treatment
- Mild cases: observe and avoid trauma
- Moderate/severe bleeding: oral steroids or IVIG
- Life-threatening bleeding: platelet transfusion and intensive care
=== Prognosis
- Most children recover within weeks to months
- Chronic ITP is less common in young children
== Nephrotic Syndrome
=== Overview
- Cardinal features
  - Massive proteinuria (nephrotic range)
  - Hypoalbuminaemia
  - Generalised oedema (periorbital, pedal, ascites)
  - Hyperlipidaemia and lipiduria
- Common causes in children
  - Minimal change disease (most common)
  - Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)
  - Membranous nephropathy (less common in children)
  - Secondary causes: infections, SLE, hepatitis, drugs
- Clinical features
  - Gradual or rapid onset generalized oedema, frothy urine, poor appetite
  - History of recurrent relapses in steroid-sensitive disease
- Physical examination
  - Periorbital puffiness, pitting oedema on legs, abdominal distension from ascites
  - Check blood pressure, signs of infection, pleural effusion
- Investigation
  - Urine dipstick: 3+ to 4+ protein; urine microscopy may show oval fat bodies
  - Spot urine protein:creatinine ratio or 24-hour urinary protein
  - Serum albumin, total protein, lipid profile, renal function tests (BUN/creatinine)
  - Complement levels and serology where indicated
- Treatment (summary and common pediatric dosing)
  - First-line: Prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day (max ~60 mg) for initial course per local protocol; then alternate-day taper
  - Supportive: salt restriction, diuretics (eg. furosemide) for severe oedema, albumin infusion with diuretic if respiratory compromise
  - Treat infections aggressively; consider pneumococcal vaccination
  - Relapse management: short steroid courses; steroid-sparing agents (cyclophosphamide, levamisole, calcineurin inhibitors, MMF) for frequent relapsers or steroid-dependence
  - Anticoagulation if thrombotic complications suspected
- Relapse definitions (concise)
  - Relapse: recurrence of significant proteinuria after remission
  - Frequent relapser: ≥2 relapses within 6 months or ≥3 within 12 months
  - Steroid-dependent: relapse while on tapering steroids or within 2 weeks of stopping
- Difference between AGN and NS (clinical pointers)
  - AGN: usually presents with hematuria, hypertension, oliguria, low complement (post-infectious)
  - NS: predominant proteinuria, severe hypoalbuminaemia, massive oedema; urine usually not frankly bloody
- Bedside/quick tests
  - Urine dipstick for protein and blood, spot protein:creatinine ratio, blood pressure measurement

== AGN
=== Overview
- Common causes
  - Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN)
  - IgA nephropathy, post-infectious GN, SLE-associated GN
- Clinical features
  - Cola- or tea-coloured urine (gross hematuria), oliguria, hypertension, oedema
- Examination
  - Measure BP, check fluid status, look for periorbital oedema
- Investigation
  - Urinalysis: hematuria with RBC casts, variable proteinuria
  - CBC, renal function, electrolytes, complement (C3 low in PSGN), ASO/anti-DNaseB titres
  - Ultrasound kidney when indicated
- Treatment
  - Mostly supportive: control hypertension, manage fluid balance and electrolytes
  - Treat underlying infection (eg. antibiotics for streptococcal infection)
  - Dialysis if severe renal failure or fluid overload unresponsive to medical therapy
- Complications
  - Acute kidney injury, hypertensive encephalopathy, pulmonary oedema
- Time course
  - Gross haematuria may persist for days–weeks; urine colour usually returns to normal over days to weeks as hematuria clears

== Cola Color Urine / High / Smokey urine
=== Overview
- Causes
  - Glomerular bleeding (eg. AGN, IgA nephropathy) → dark/tea/cola-coloured urine
  - Lower urinary tract bleeding (stones, UTI, trauma) → fresh red blood and clots
  - Myoglobinuria (rhabdomyolysis) or hemoglobinuria (hemolysis) → brownish urine without RBCs
- Key differentiators (clinical)
  - Glomerular source: cola-colour, microscopic dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, proteinuria, hypertension
  - Non-glomerular: visible clots, severe flank pain, normal BP, minimal proteinuria
- Initial workup
  - Urinalysis and microscopy, urine dipstick, CBC, renal function tests, ultrasound KUB if indicated
- Immediate red flags requiring urgent referral
  - Oliguria/anuria, rapidly rising creatinine, uncontrolled hypertension, severe anemia, gross persistent haematuria with clots

== Bowing of Legs, Knock Knee - Rickets (2ta picture)
=== Vitamin D synthesis
- Skin: 7-dehydrocholesterol → cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) by UVB (290–315 nm)
- Liver hydroxylation → 25(OH)D (calcidiol)
- Kidney hydroxylation → 1,25(OH)2D (calcitriol) — active form
=== Deficiency changes
- Impaired mineralisation of osteoid → soft bones, widening of metaphyses
- Hypocalcaemia (tetany in severe cases), secondary hyperparathyroidism
=== Clinical features of rickets
- Age: classic between 6 months and 2 years (rapid growth)
- Bony deformities: genu varum (bow legs), genu valgum (knock knees), wrist widening, rachitic rosary
- Delayed motor milestones, hypotonia
- Dental enamel defects, delayed tooth eruption
=== Investigation and X-ray findings
- Serum: low 25(OH)D, low/normal calcium, low phosphate, raised ALP, raised PTH
- X-ray: widening, cupping and fraying of metaphyses (wrist, knee), osteopenia
- Differential: renal rickets (check renal function), hypophosphataemic rickets (low phosphate)
=== Treatment
- Nutritional rickets: Oral vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) 2000 IU daily for 6 weeks OR stoss therapy per local protocol; maintenance 400–600 IU/day
- Calcium supplementation if dietary intake low (eg. elemental calcium 500–1000 mg/day depending on age)
- Correct severe hypocalcaemia urgently (IV calcium gluconate) with cardiac monitoring
- Treat underlying cause (eg. malabsorption)
=== Prevention
- Exclusive breastfeeding with vitamin D supplementation for breastfed infants (400 IU/day from birth)
- Maternal supplementation in pregnancy and lactation where indicated
- Sunlight exposure and dietary sources (fortified milk, fish)
== Gum bleeding - Acute leukaemia, Aplastic anaemia, ITP, Scurvy
=== Causes of gum bleeding
- Acute leukaemia
- Aplastic anaemia
- ITP
- Scurvy
=== Acute leukaemia
- Clinical features
  - Pallor, fatigue, fever, recurrent infections, easy bruising, gum hypertrophy/bleeding
  - Bone pain, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly
- Examination
  - Pallor, petechiae, gingival hypertrophy, hepatosplenomegaly
- Investigation with interpretation
  - CBC: cytopenias or leukocytosis with blasts
  - Peripheral smear: circulating blasts
  - Bone marrow aspirate/biopsy: >25% blasts (confirmatory)
  - Immunophenotyping (flow cytometry) for lineage
- Confirmatory test
  - Bone marrow morphology + immunophenotyping, cytogenetics/ molecular studies for risk stratification
- Treatment
  - Referral to oncology; combination chemotherapy per protocol, supportive transfusions, treat infections, tumor lysis precautions
=== Aplastic anaemia
- Aetiology
  - Idiopathic, drugs, toxins, viral (hepatitis, EBV), autoimmune
- Clinical features
  - Progressive pallor, mucosal bleeding, infections due to neutropenia
- Investigation
  - Pancytopenia on CBC, hypocellular marrow on bone marrow biopsy (dry tap may occur)
- Treatment
  - Immunosuppressive therapy (eg. ATG + cyclosporine) or bone marrow transplant if severe and donor available; supportive transfusions and infection control
=== ITP
- Aetiology and pathogenesis
  - Autoimmune platelet destruction often following viral infection; antiplatelet antibodies
- Clinical features
  - Petechiae, purpura, mucosal bleeding (gum, epistaxis), usually well child otherwise
- Investigation
  - Isolated thrombocytopenia on CBC, normal WBC and Hb; peripheral smear with reduced platelets
  - Exclude secondary causes (HIV, HCV, SLE)
- Treatment
  - Observe if platelet >30k and no significant bleeding
  - For mucosal or significant bleeding: oral steroids (eg. prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day short course) or IVIG (0.8–1 g/kg once) for rapid rise
  - Refractory/chronic: consider splenectomy or thrombopoietin receptor agonists in older children
=== Scurvy (Vitamin C deficiency)
- Pathogenesis
  - Impaired collagen synthesis → fragile capillaries, poor wound healing
- Clinical features
  - Gingival swelling and bleeding, perifollicular haemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing, bone pain, irritability
- Investigation
  - Clinical diagnosis supported by low vitamin C level (if available) and resolution with treatment
- Treatment
  - Vitamin C 100–300 mg/day divided (children dosing adjusted by age) until recovery then maintenance; improve diet (citrus fruits, tomatoes, green vegetables)
=== Comparison
- Key differences
  - Acute leukaemia: pancytopenia/blasts, systemic features (fever, bone pain)
  - Aplastic anaemia: pancytopenia with hypocellular marrow, infections common
  - ITP: isolated thrombocytopenia, otherwise well child
  - Scurvy: dietary history, periodontal disease, skin findings, good response to vitamin C
== Palmar Pallor (IDA)
=== Palmar pallor in IDA, thalassemia, leukemia, aplastic anemia
 - Palmar pallor is a bedside sign indicating anaemia but not its cause; use history, growth and associated signs to narrow differential before laboratory testing
 - Key discriminators: chronicity, growth parameters, family history (thalassaemia), bleeding/bruising (leukemia/aplastic), nutritional history (IDA)
=== IDA
- Causes
  - Nutritional iron deficiency, chronic blood loss, hookworm, malaria, hemoglobinopathies differential
- Aetiology
  - Inadequate intake, increased requirement (growth), malabsorption, chronic blood loss
- Clinical features
  - Pallor (conjunctival/palmar), fatigue, pica, breathlessness, tachycardia
- Investigation: peripheral blood film
  - CBC: microcytic hypochromic anemia, low MCV, low ferritin, high TIBC
  - Peripheral smear: microcytosis, hypochromia, anisopoikilocytosis
- Treatment
  - Oral elemental iron 3–6 mg/kg/day in divided doses (or 2–3 mg/kg elemental iron once daily for adherence) for at least 3 months after correction of Hb
  - Treat underlying cause (deworming, diet counselling)
  - Consider parenteral iron for malabsorption or intolerance
=== Vitamin C Deficiency
==== Scurvy
- Cause
  - Dietary deficiency of ascorbic acid (vitamin C)
- Clinical features
  - Gingival swelling/bleeding, mucosal bleeding, petechiae, corkscrew hairs, joint pain, delayed wound healing
- Differential diagnosis
  - Hematologic bleeding disorders, scurvy distinguished by nutritional history and skin changes
- X-ray findings
  - Subperiosteal hemorrhages, metaphyseal lines (white line of Frankel), scorbutic zone
- Treatment
  - As above: vitamin C replacement and dietary correction
== Severe Dehydration
=== Overview
- Key management points (concise)
- IMCI classification: some dehydration, severe dehydration, no dehydration — follow Plan B/C accordingly
- Plan C (severe): IV access and isotonic fluid bolus (eg. Ringer's lactate or normal saline 20 ml/kg bolus repeat as needed) and monitor for signs of overhydration
- ORS for some dehydration: 75 ml/kg over 4 hours (adjust by age per IMCI chart in table)
- Zinc: 20 mg daily for 10–14 days (10 mg for infants < 6 months)
- Antibiotics: only for dysentery, suspected cholera with severe dehydration, or systemic infection
- Monitor electrolytes and correct clinically significant hypo/hypernatremia carefully
== Congenital Hemolytic Anaemia due to Thalassemia
=== Overview
- Definitive treatment: allogenic bone marrow transplant from HLA-identical donor
- Regular blood transfusion + iron chelation
- Splenectomy may be needed
- Consanguineous marriage risk
=== Prerequisites of splenectomy
- Age > 5 years
- Vaccination against pneumococcus, H. influenzae, meningococcus at least 4 weeks prior
- Lifelong prophylactic penicillin therapy
- HBV and chicken pox vaccines
=== Labs
- PBF: microcytic hypochromic anemia
- Hb electrophoresis (confirmatory) → HbF = 98%
- Serum iron profile
  - TIBC decreased
  - Percentage saturation increased
- X-ray skull: hair-on-end appearance
=== Discussion points
- Disease type: β-thalassemia major vs intermedia vs trait
- Facial deformity: marrow expansion in skull and maxilla → frontal bossing, chipmunk facies
- Why deformities occur: extramedullary hematopoiesis and marrow expansion due to ineffective erythropoiesis
- Types of thalassemia: α vs β and severity spectrum (major, intermedia, trait)
- Pathogenesis: defective globin chain synthesis causing hemolysis and ineffective erythropoiesis
- Clinical features: anemia, growth retardation, jaundice, splenomegaly
- Investigation: CBC (microcytic hypochromic), Hb electrophoresis (raised HbF, HbA2), peripheral smear with target cells
- Treatment
  - Regular transfusions to maintain target pre-transfusion Hb (eg. >9–10 g/dL in major)
  - Iron chelation (eg. deferasirox, deferoxamine) to prevent iron overload
  - Hematopoietic stem cell transplant is curative if matched donor available
- Transfusion indications
  - Severe symptomatic anemia, growth failure, cardiac decompensation; individualized protocols
- Why iron is withheld and chelators used
  - Repeated transfusions cause iron overload → organ damage; chelators remove excess iron
- Splenectomy indications, prerequisites, and aftercare
  - Indicated for hypersplenism with increased transfusion requirement; delay until after age 5 when possible
  - Ensure pre-op vaccinations and penicillin prophylaxis post-op
- Why the X-ray shows hair-on-end appearance
  - Prominent vertical trabeculae from marrow hyperplasia
- Difference between IDA and thalassemia
  - IDA: low ferritin, high TIBC, severe microcytosis with low RBC count
  - Thalassemia trait: normal/high ferritin, normal TIBC, higher RBC count relative to Hb, target cells, Hb electrophoresis abnormal
=== X-ray
- Skull lateral view: widening of diploic space, thinning of outer table, thickening and coarsening of trabeculae giving hair-on-end appearance
=== Mongoloid facies
- Frontal bossing
- Epicanthal fold
- Depressed nasal bridge
- Prominent zygoma
- Underdeveloped jaw
- Malocclusion of teeth
== Jaundice - Acute viral hepatitis
=== Overview
- Acute viral hepatitis causes liver inflammation and jaundice; in children, HAV and HBV are most common, but HCV and HEV also occur
- Non-viral causes of paediatric jaundice include hemolysis, metabolic disease, drug-induced liver injury and cholestatic disorders
=== Causes
- Viral hepatitis: HAV, HBV, HCV, HEV
- Other hepatic causes: autoimmune hepatitis, drug-induced liver injury, metabolic liver disease
- Pre-hepatic: hemolysis, sepsis
=== Transmission
- HAV/HEV: fecal-oral spread
- HBV/HCV: blood/bodily fluids, perinatal and horizontal transmission
- Prevention: vaccination, hygiene, safe blood and infection control
=== Clinical features
- Fever, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dark urine, pale stools, jaundice, hepatomegaly
- Children often have milder symptoms, but severe disease can present with coagulopathy, encephalopathy or acute liver failure
=== Investigation
- Serum LFTs: AST, ALT, total and direct bilirubin, ALP, GGT, albumin
- Coagulation: INR, PT
- Viral serology: HAV IgM, HBsAg, anti-HBs, anti-HBc IgM/IgG, HBeAg, anti-HCV, HEV IgM as available
- Additional tests: ultrasound abdomen, autoimmune markers, metabolic screen if indicated
=== Management
- Supportive care: hydration, rest, nutrition, avoid hepatotoxic drugs and medications
- Specific therapy: antiviral treatment per local protocol for chronic HBV/HCV, consider ribavirin or antivirals for select HEV cases in immunosuppressed patients
- Monitor for signs of fulminant hepatic failure: worsening coagulopathy, encephalopathy, rising bilirubin; refer early for specialist care
=== HBV markers interpretation
- HBsAg: current infection
- Anti-HBs: immunity from vaccination or recovery
- Anti-HBc IgM: acute/recent infection
- Anti-HBc IgG: past or chronic infection
- HBeAg: high infectivity
== Neonatal jaundice
=== Overview
- Neonatal jaundice is common but must be classified as physiologic or pathologic based on timing, bilirubin rise and clinical features
=== Causes by age
- First 24 hrs: suggest pathological causes such as hemolysis, sepsis, Rh/ABO incompatibility, congenital infection, metabolic disease
- Days 2–7: usually physiologic jaundice in term infants; may also include breast milk jaundice
- Beyond 14 days: pathological prolonged jaundice causes such as cholestasis, hypothyroidism, biliary atresia
=== Red flags for pathological neonatal jaundice
- Onset < 24 hrs of life
- Bilirubin rise >5 mg/dL/day or bilirubin above treatment threshold for age in hours
- Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, dark urine, pale stools
- Signs of sepsis, poor feeding, lethargy, encephalopathy
=== Investigation
- Total and direct bilirubin, blood group and Coombs test
- CBC with reticulocyte count, peripheral smear
- G6PD assay, thyroid function tests, LFTs, urine reducing substances when indicated
- Use age-in-hours treatment nomograms for phototherapy and exchange transfusion decisions
=== Management
- Phototherapy according to age-specific thresholds and risk factors
- Exchange transfusion for extreme bilirubin levels or acute bilirubin encephalopathy risk
- IVIG for isoimmune hemolytic disease with significant hyperbilirubinemia
- Treat underlying cause: infection, dehydration, breastfeeding issues, metabolic disease
== Neonatal sepsis
=== Overview
- Neonatal sepsis is a systemic infection in the first 28 days of life; early onset is often vertically transmitted, late onset is often hospital- or community-acquired
=== Risk factors
- Prematurity, low birth weight, PROM, maternal fever, chorioamnionitis, unsterile delivery, invasive procedures
=== Clinical features
- Temperature instability, poor feeding, lethargy, respiratory distress, apnea, vomiting, abdominal distension, jaundice, irritability
=== Investigation
- Blood culture, CBC, CRP, blood glucose, blood gas
- Lumbar puncture if safe and indicated, chest X-ray, urine culture for late-onset sepsis
=== Treatment
- Empirical antibiotics after cultures: ampicillin + gentamicin ± cefotaxime per local protocol
- Supportive care: IV fluids, oxygen/ventilation, inotropes, glucose management
- Adjust antibiotics according to culture and sensitivity results
== Congenital hypothyroidism
=== Overview
- Primary congenital hypothyroidism due to thyroid dysgenesis or dyshormonogenesis; can be transient (maternal antibodies, iodine imbalance)
=== Clinical features
- Poor feeding, prolonged neonatal jaundice, constipation
- Lethargy, hypotonia, poor weight gain, coarse facies, macroglossia, umbilical hernia, hoarse cry
=== Screening & Diagnosis
- Newborn screening: TSH (primary screen) and/or T4 depending on programme
- Confirm with serum free T4 and TSH if screen abnormal
- Thyroid imaging (ultrasound/scan) if structural anomaly suspected
=== Treatment
- Start levothyroxine urgently when diagnosis confirmed: 10–15 mcg/kg/day in neonates (adjust by weight/age)
- Monitor TFTs at 2 and 4 weeks after start, then every 1–3 months in infancy
- Early treatment prevents neurodevelopmental impairment
== Down syndrome
=== Overview
- Trisomy 21: common chromosomal disorder with characteristic phenotype and multisystem involvement
=== Key features
- Low set ears, epicanthic folds, flat nasal bridge, single palmar crease, sandal gap, generalized hypotonia, small upslanting palpebral fissures
=== Associated conditions
- Cardiac: AV septal defect, VSD, PDA
- GI: duodenal atresia, Hirschsprung disease
- Endocrine: hypothyroidism, growth failure
- Hematologic: increased risk of ALL
- Developmental: intellectual disability, speech and motor delay
=== Diagnosis and screening
- Prenatal screening: NT scan, combined first-trimester screen, cell-free DNA
- Confirmatory: karyotype from peripheral blood
=== Management
- Multidisciplinary care: cardiology, ENT, audiology, ophthalmology, endocrinology, early intervention and educational support
- Routine screening: echo at diagnosis, thyroid annually, hearing and vision checks
== TOF - Central cyanosis (tongue) & clubbed and cyanosed fingernails
=== Overview
- Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF): VSD, overriding aorta, RVOT obstruction (infundibular stenosis), right ventricular hypertrophy
=== Clinical features
- Central cyanosis, clubbing (older children), harsh systolic ejection murmur, cyanotic (tet) spells with irritability and syncope
=== Cyanotic (hypercyanotic) spell management
- Immediate: calm child, knee-to-chest position (increases SVR), give 100% oxygen, ensure IV access
- Medical: give IV fluids bolus, give morphine 0.1 mg/kg IV to reduce infundibular spasm, consider propranolol (0.01–0.1 mg/kg IV) or oral beta-blocker if recurrent
- If persistent: phenylephrine to increase systemic vascular resistance may help; urgent paediatric cardiology input
=== Investigation
- ECG: right axis deviation, RVH
- Chest X-ray: boot-shaped heart
- Echocardiography: diagnostic and defines anatomy
=== Definitive treatment
- Surgical repair (complete intracardiac repair) usually in infancy; palliative BT shunt if not suitable for early repair
== Bulged anterior fontanelle
=== Overview
- A tense or bulging anterior fontanelle suggests raised intracranial pressure in an infant and needs urgent evaluation
=== Causes
- Meningitis, intracranial hemorrhage, hydrocephalus, space-occupying lesion, metabolic encephalopathy
=== Assessment and investigation
- Urgent clinical assessment: level of consciousness, bulbar signs, head circumference, focal neurology
- Neuroimaging (cranial ultrasound in neonates/infants, CT/MRI) before lumbar puncture if raised ICP suspected
- Lumbar puncture only if safe (no mass effect) to diagnose meningitis; send CSF for cell count, glucose, protein, culture
=== Management
- Stabilize airway/breathing/circulation, start empirical IV antibiotics for suspected bacterial meningitis after appropriate samples, manage raised ICP (head elevation, hyperosmolar therapy) and urgent neurosurgical/neurology referral
== Diabetes
=== Overview
- Diabetes mellitus in children: mainly type 1 autoimmune β-cell destruction; type 2 increasing with obesity
=== Presentation
- Polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, nocturnal enuresis, DKA in acute presentation
=== DKA features and management (summary)
- Features: dehydration, Kussmaul respiration, abdominal pain, vomiting, altered sensorium
- Initial: cautious fluid resuscitation (bolus 10–20 ml/kg isotonic saline if shock), correct dehydration gradually; check electrolytes and potassium before insulin
- Insulin: start after initial fluid resuscitation; IV insulin infusion 0.05–0.1 units/kg/hr (no bolus in children) and adjust per glucose
- Avoid rapid correction of hyperglycaemia to reduce risk of cerebral oedema; monitor neurological status closely
- Replace potassium when serum K normalises or low and patient producing urine
=== Long-term management
- Basal-bolus insulin regimens, carbohydrate counting, education, DKA prevention, monitor HbA1c and growth
== Monoarthritis
=== Differential
- Infectious septic arthritis, reactive arthritis, juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA), haemarthrosis (haemophilia), rheumatic fever (polyarthritis)
=== Rheumatic fever (brief)
- Jones criteria: major (carditis, polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules) and minor criteria; evidence of preceding streptococcal infection
- Presentation: migratory large-joint arthritis, carditis, chorea
- Investigation: ASO/anti-DNase B, ECG, echo for carditis, inflammatory markers
- Treatment: anti-inflammatory (aspirin or naproxen), antibiotics (penicillin), manage cardiac complications
=== Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA)
- Types: oligoarticular, polyarticular, systemic onset (Still disease)
- Clinical: persistent arthritis >6 weeks, systemic features in systemic onset
- Investigation: exclude infection, CBC, ESR/CRP, ANA (uveitis risk), imaging
- Treatment: NSAIDs, intra-articular steroids, DMARDs (methotrexate), biologics for refractory cases
=== Haemophilia (brief)
- X-linked bleeding disorder (factor VIII in haemophilia A, factor IX in B)
- Presents with joint swelling, haemarthrosis after minor trauma
- Diagnosis: prolonged APTT, specific factor assay
- Treatment: factor replacement (on-demand or prophylaxis), desmopressin for mild haemophilia A, avoid NSAIDs; RICE for acute joint bleeds